\details{
The function reads \code{mRates.txt} and \code{qRates.txt} and adds the following
node attributes (with \verb{_<suffix>} appended to each name if \code{suffix} is
given):
\itemize{
\item \code{m_mean}: posterior mean of the migration rate \eqn{m} (linear scale).
\item \code{log10m_mean}, \code{log10m_sd}: posterior mean and standard deviation of
\eqn{\log_{10} m}.
\item \code{m_p_above}: proportion of draws in which the deme's \eqn{\log_{10} m} is
above the average over all demes of that draw (as in the MAPS and EEMS
"sign" plots).
\item \code{m_sign}: \code{"above"} if \code{m_p_above} > 0.95, \code{"below"} if \code{m_p_above} <
0.05, and \code{"uncertain"} otherwise.
\item \code{log10N_mean}, \code{log10N_sd}, \code{N_p_above}: the same summaries for
\eqn{\log_{10} N = -\log_{10}(2) - \log_{10} q}, so that larger values
mean larger populations.
\item \code{m_colour} (only if \code{colors = TRUE}): the color of each node, see below.
}

Existing attributes with these names are replaced. Attributes describe the
posterior at each deme; values at demes far from any sample mostly reflect
the prior, which \code{m_sign} and \code{m_p_above} help to identify.

If \code{costs = TRUE}, edge costs are set to the inverse of the MAPS edge
migration rate. MAPS sets the rate of the edge between demes \eqn{a} and
\eqn{b} to \eqn{(m_a + m_b)/2}; since this is linear in the node rates,
its posterior mean is the mean of the two \code{m_mean} values, and the cost is
\eqn{2/(m_a + m_b)} using those posterior means. High migration thus means
low cost.

If \code{colors = TRUE}, nodes are colored on a continuous diverging scale by
\code{log10m_mean} relative to its average over all demes: red below, white at
and blue above the average, with limits symmetric around the average.
Demes where \code{m_sign} is \code{"uncertain"} are grey. The colors are stored in
the attribute \code{m_colour} and color rules are set on it.
A \code{\link{gGraph}} holds one set of costs and one set of color rules,
so these replace any existing ones.
